3.4.1
===================
This release introduces native shell autocompletions, visual highlights for
destructive commands, and a configuration fix for terminal keyboard enhancements
on macOS, alongside other minor improvements.

Destructive Command Highlighting
The TUI now automatically highlights known destructive commands in red to
provide a clear visual warning before execution (#54).

Shell Autocompletions
Native shell completion scripts are now available for all currently supported
shells, improving command-line interaction and discovery directly from your
prompt.

Keyboard Enhancement Compatibility
Advanced terminal keyboard protocols are now disabled by default on
macOS to prevent interference with certain native keyboard layouts. A new
tui.keyboard_enhancement configuration option has been introduced, allowing
users to manually enable or disable this protocol as needed.

Configuration Directory Initialization
Fixed an issue where the configuration editor failed to open if the target
directory did not exist (#50). The application now ensures the directory is
created before launching the editor.


3.4.0
===================
New changelog Subcommand
Designed to eliminate context switching, this command allows you to read the
release notes directly in your terminal. After checking for or performing an
update, you can now immediately review what changed without opening a browser.
It includes filtering options to list releases by version range (--from, --to)
or type (--major, --minor).

Comprehensive Update Summary
The update command now aggregates and displays the Update Considerations for
all versions covered during the update process, ensuring you don't miss critical
instructions even when skipping multiple versions.

Fine-Tune Suggestions
In the variable replacement TUI, you can now press the Right Arrow key on a
suggestion to copy it into the input field. This makes it easy to fine-tune or
slightly modify a value before confirming it.
